    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the help. i checked the pot and the drainage seems okay, but the soil does stay pretty damp for a long time even after a week. i'm also worried it might be a nutrient thing since the yellowing is spreading. should i try adding some fertilizer or is that gonna make the root issue worse?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the soil is staying damp for too long, that's the primary culprit, and adding fertilizer right now could definitely make things worse for your struggling roots. It's best to address the watering issue first, as stressed roots can't absorb nutrients properly anyway. Once you've let the soil dry out more consistently, *then* you can start a gentle feeding regimen.\n\nThink of it like this: if the roots are drowning, they can't even process the nutrients, no matter how much you sprinkle them on top. Give your Monstera a chance to recover from the dampness before you introduce any more food. If the yellowing continues after you adjust your watering habits, then a very diluted fertilizer might be the next step.",
